A stack is the right choice for an undo/redo system because actions need to be handled in Last-In, First-Out (LIFO) order. The most recent action should be the first action that gets undone. For example, if a user performs three actions, the third action should be undone before the second or first action. A stack makes this process simple because the `push()` method adds an action to the top, while `pop()` removes the most recent action. The redo stack also follows this same structure. When an action is undone, it is moved to the redo stack. If the user performs a new action, the redo stack is cleared because the previous redo history is no longer valid.

A queue is better suited for a help desk because customers should be helped in the order they arrive. This follows the First-In, First-Out (FIFO) structure. The first customer who enters the queue should be the first customer helped. The `enqueue()` method adds customers to the rear, while `dequeue()` removes customers from the front. The `peek()` method allows the program to see who is next without removing them.

These custom implementations differ from Python's built-in lists because they specifically control how data can be accessed. A list can add, remove, or access elements at different positions. The custom stack only provides stack operations, while the custom queue only provides queue operations. Using nodes and pointers also demonstrates how these data structures work internally instead of relying on Python's built-in list behavior. This makes the structures reusable and helps demonstrate why different data structures are useful for different problems.
